@page "/docs/common/downloads"

@* Downloads: every way IzzyBlazor hands the user a file, in one place. A DataGrid's export links to
   endpoints you map (DataExport's Excel and CSV writers, DataExport.Read for any other format, a
   PDF recipe with QuestPDF), a chart's Download (a PNG drawn in the browser, chart-export.ts), a
   chart's PNG posted in a form for a PDF the server builds, and printing. The PDF endpoint was
   under Data from an API's "Export files" until Nov 10, 2026. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="downloads">
    <Lead>
        A grid's rows download as Excel, CSV or PDF from endpoints you map. A chart downloads as a PNG that the browser
        creates. To put a chart in a PDF, send its picture to your server in a form.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Exporting a grid's rows</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To add an Export button to a DataGrid or TreeList, set <Code>ExportExcelHref</Code> or <Code>ExportCsvHref</Code>
            to an endpoint you map. The link contains the grid's <Code>filter</Code>, <Code>sort</Code>, <Code>search</Code>
            and <Code>columns</Code>. On the server, <Code>DataExport.WriteExcelAsync</Code> and <Code>WriteCsvAsync</Code>
            write those rows, and stream them as they're read:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)">@Excel</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The file contains the rows the user sees, from every page, and the columns shown, in their order.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>It's a link, so it works on static pages and with JavaScript off.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The fields you pass to <Code>DataExport</Code> set what can be written, whatever the URL asks for. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/components/data-grid/export">DataGrid export</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The fields need the grid's keys. If the grid sets <Code>FilterCase</Code>, set the same value as each
                field's <Code>Case</Code>. Otherwise the file can contain different rows.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>For a TreeList, pass <Code>tree:</Code> to write the rows in the tree's order, with their levels.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Other formats</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            For a format the library doesn't write, <Code>DataExport.Read</Code> returns the same columns and rows that the
            Excel and CSV writers get, and you write the file. This PDF endpoint uses
            <Anchor Href="https://www.questpdf.com" External>QuestPDF</Anchor>, but any PDF library works the same way. To
            list the format on the grid, add <Code>&lt;DataGridExportFormat Label="Export PDF" Href="/orders/export.pdf" /&gt;</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="10,11">@Pdf</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A PDF is laid out all at once, so this endpoint reads every row first. Where a filter can match millions of rows, cap the rows as this endpoint does.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The query runs when the rows are read. An endpoint that streams the file, as CSV does, reads the first row
                before it starts the response. So if the query fails, the endpoint can still return an error.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>Each column's <Code>Key</Code> is its name in a keyed format such as JSON. Its <Code>Text</Code> formats a value the way CSV does.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>QuestPDF is free under its Community license for organizations below $1M in revenue, and paid above it.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                For rows read by <Code>DynamicColumn</Code>, build the fields with <Code>DataFields.ByName</Code>, by the same
                names and types, so the keys and values match the grid's:
                <Code>DataFields.ByName&lt;IDictionary&lt;string, object?&gt;&gt;("Margin %", typeof(double))</Code>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Saving a chart as a PNG</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To add a button that saves a chart as a PNG, set <Code>Download</Code>. The button appears by the chart's title,
            and the file is named after the title. In a Tile's or Card's header, <Code>&lt;ChartDownload /&gt;</Code> adds
            the same button for the chart in it, or for the chart its <Code>For</Code> names. To use a button of your own,
            set its <Code>data-ib-chart-download</Code> to the chart's <Code>id</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@ChartButtons</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser creates the picture at twice the chart's size on screen, in the page's current color scheme. It works
            on static pages too. With JavaScript off, there's no picture. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/chart/download">Chart download</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Putting a chart in a PDF</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To put a chart in a PDF your server builds, send its picture in a form. Add a file input and set its
            <Code>data-ib-chart-png</Code> to the chart's <Code>id</Code>. When the form is sent, the input receives the
            chart's PNG, and the endpoint reads it like any uploaded file:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Revenue.razor" HighlightLines="3">@ChartForm</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="3,16">@ChartPdf</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The form is sent as <Code>multipart/form-data</Code>, which a file requires, whatever its <Code>enctype</Code> is set to.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The form's other fields are sent too, such as a date range or a note for the report. For several charts, add an input for each.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Leave out <Code>data-enhance</Code>, because the response is a file, not a page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>With JavaScript off, or when the chart couldn't be drawn, the input is empty and the endpoint receives no file. This endpoint returns an error, but it could build the PDF without the chart instead.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Printing</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser's Print command prints the page. Its Save as PDF option creates a PDF with no code on the server.
            Charts and tables print as they appear on screen.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Excel = """
        app.MapGet("/orders/export.xlsx", (HttpContext context, ShopDb db) => Results.Stream(
            stream => DataExport.WriteExcelAsync(stream, db.Orders.AsNoTracking(), context.Request.QueryString.Value,
                DataFields.Of<Order>(), "Orders", cancellationToken: context.RequestAborted),
            "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet", "orders.xlsx"));
        """;

    private const string Pdf = """
        using QuestPDF.Fluent;
        using QuestPDF.Infrastructure;

        QuestPDF.Settings.License = LicenseType.Community;

        app.MapGet("/orders/export.pdf", async (HttpContext context, ShopDb db) =>
        {
            var orders = db.Orders.AsNoTracking()
                .Select(o => new OrderRow { Id = o.Id, Customer = o.Customer.Name, Total = o.Total });
            var export = DataExport.Read(orders, context.Request.QueryString.Value,
                DataFields.Of<OrderRow>(), context.RequestAborted);
            var rows = new List<object?[]>();
            await foreach (var row in export.Rows)
            {
                if (rows.Count == 5_000)
                {
                    return Results.Problem("Narrow the filter: a PDF holds at most 5,000 orders.", statusCode: 413);
                }

                rows.Add(row);
            }

            var pdf = Document.Create(document => document.Page(page =>
            {
                page.Margin(36);
                page.Content().Table(table =>
                {
                    table.ColumnsDefinition(columns => export.Columns.ToList().ForEach(_ => columns.RelativeColumn()));
                    table.Header(header => export.Columns.ToList().ForEach(c => header.Cell().Text(c.Caption).Bold()));
                    foreach (var row in rows)
                    {
                        for (var i = 0; i < row.Length; i++)
                        {
                            table.Cell().PaddingVertical(2).Text(export.Columns[i].Text(row[i]));
                        }
                    }
                });
            })).GeneratePdf();
            return Results.File(pdf, "application/pdf", "orders.pdf");
        });
        """;

    private const string ChartButtons = """
        <Chart Items="quarters" Argument="q => q.Quarter" Title="Revenue by region, 2026" Download>
            ...
        </Chart>

        <Button StartIcon="Lucide.Download" data-ib-chart-download="signups">Download</Button>
        <Chart id="signups" Items="days" Argument="d => d.Day" Title="New signups, last 7 days">
            ...
        </Chart>
        """;

    private const string ChartForm = """
        <form method="post" action="/reports/revenue.pdf">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="file" name="chart" data-ib-chart-png="revenue" hidden />
            <Button Submit StartIcon="Lucide.FileDown">Download PDF</Button>
        </form>

        <Chart id="revenue" Items="quarters" Argument="q => q.Quarter" Title="Revenue by region, 2026">
            ...
        </Chart>
        """;

    private const string ChartPdf = """
        using QuestPDF.Fluent;

        app.MapPost("/reports/revenue.pdf", async (IFormFile? chart) =>
        {
            if (chart is null)
            {
                return Results.BadRequest("The chart's picture didn't come with the form.");
            }

            using var image = new MemoryStream();
            await chart.CopyToAsync(image);
            var pdf = Document.Create(document => document.Page(page =>
            {
                page.Margin(36);
                page.Header().Text("Revenue report, 2026").FontSize(18).Bold();
                page.Content().PaddingTop(12).Image(image.ToArray());
            })).GeneratePdf();
            return Results.File(pdf, "application/pdf", "revenue.pdf");
        });
        """;
}
